[{:seon.task/name "an-agents-effect-budget-survives-a-restart"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. Found 2026-11-11 by the Data model session on Context's finding #9 (a render of spend): the effect owner's spend per budget key (seon.fx's proc state :spent) starts {} with each owner process (fx.clj owner-proc's init), is only added to by charge and read by admit, and nothing restores it from the store, so an agent's own budget (my.fx/call with {:budget n}, keyed by the agent's id in seon.agent-proc) starts over at a root restart, its spend forgotten. Scale: no production code sets a budget; only an agent on its own effects. The class: a decision read from a counter only one process remembers, where the store already holds the facts it sums (each model call is a :seon.model-call entity holding its agent and its tokens; prices are config). Change: admission reads the spend the store holds for the key (the sum over the key's calls, computed where read, never stored: no derived value in the store), and the :spent counter goes; a non-model effect that charges cost states it as its own fact first, or is not budgeted. Cost: n = the key's calls; one pull a request admitted with a budget, none without. Proof, red first: seon.fx-test/an-agents-budget-holds-across-a-restart (spend a budget, restart the owner, the next call over it is refused :seon.fx/budget-spent; red today: admitted)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "deps-at-the-repl"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the :deps effect (`seon.fx/resolve-deps`) resolves a deps map through Jolt's ``jolt.deps/resolve-deps`` and answers the source roots or :seon.fx/unresolved; there is no add-lib or sync-deps at the REPL and no checksum (docs/source.md#dependencies; leaves them out). Correct, with Clojure's names (clojure.repl.deps): add-lib, add-libs and sync-deps ask the :deps effect through `my.fx/call`, load the answered roots in the agent's process and record the coordinate on its branch; a resolved jar or git tree is checked against a sha256 recorded at its first resolve, and a mismatch is the effect's typed error."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/add-lib-loads-a-resolved-library seon.fx-test/a-changed-artifact-is-refused-by-its-checksum}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-effect-opens-a-receipt"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an agent holds an effect only as the answer of the eval that asked, once it comes (no effect fact), so an agent that dies with a :file, :git or :model call in flight leaves no record that it asked, and a resumed branch cannot tell sent from unsent. v1 committed a receipt before the handler ran, settled it once, and never re-sent it on recovery (~/src/seon/src/seon/effect.clj). Correct: for a non-idempotent kind, the agent writes an effect fact (:seon.fx/status :open, declared then) with kind, request and the form's ordinal on its branch before `my.fx/call` sends; the answer settles that entity and links the eval; the reconciler marks a dead agent's open receipt :interrupted and never re-sends it. Foundation for `seon.fx/files-and-shell-for-any-work`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-dead-agents-open-effect-is-interrupted-not-resent seon.fx-test/an-effect-links-its-eval}}
 {:seon.task/name "files-and-shell-for-any-work"
  :seon.task/plan "Seon does any work its user wants (AGENTS §1; owner 2026-10-22). What exists: the effect kinds in resources/seon/system.edn :fx are :echo :file :source :git :deps :build :model; `seon.fx/write-file` writes any path with no prior-digest check; there is no my.fs, my.edit or my.shell. Correct: :fs (read, glob, stat, write with the prior digest as precondition), :edit (exact-span text and line windows) and :shell (argv only, cwd, an env allowlist, a stdin cap, bounded by bytes not a clock) kinds, with my.fs, my.edit and my.shell as thin wrappers over `my.fx/call`. v1's handler policy becomes config keys (:seon.config.fs/*, :seon.config.shell/env-allowlist; v1 src/seon/fs/jvm.clj, shell/jvm.clj). Each function gets a section in docs/agent.md. Deletes: write-file's unchecked path, folded into the :fs write."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.fx/an-effect-opens-a-receipt"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-write-needs-the-prior-digest seon.fx-test/shell-answers-exit-and-digested-output seon.fx-test/shell-sees-only-allowed-env}}
 {:seon.task/name "web-fetch-and-search"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: no :web effect, so an agent cannot fetch a page or search (parity.md gap 11). v1: `my.web/fetch` and `search`, http(s) only, no userinfo in the URL, manual redirects with loop detection, a body cap (~/src/seon/src/seon/web/jvm.clj); the search key held by root, named by an env-var config key. Correct: :web-fetch and :web-search kinds in system.edn :fx, the search key read only by root's owner (as `seon.fx/model` reads the model key), with my.web as a wrapper over `my.fx/call`; caps as :seon.config.web/* keys."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.fx/an-effect-opens-a-receipt"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/redirects-are-bounded seon.fx-test/search-key-stays-in-root}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tree-run-is-one-function"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the one-offs survey (the archived paper one-offs-2026-10-23.md (git history); owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs, DECISIONS). Item 5. What exists: `seon.fx/build` and `seon.fx/run-on-checkout` (the :tests effect's run, `seon.fx/run-tests`) repeat one mechanism (the `child-env-scrub` env, `bin/worktree --at`, a log file, a niced command in that tree, the cleanup). Done since: `seon.fx/test-results` reads the stored results at each test's digest (`seon.fx/stored-results`, seon.proofs' verdicts), no longer prove's stdout. Correct: one private in-tree runner both call. Risk: the two differ in where the tree lives (generations-dir against tests-dir) and in what they keep after (a build's tree stays, a test run's is removed)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/seon-fx-build-run-checkout-call-private-tree}}
 {:seon.task/name "tree-id-and-inputs-spawn-only-what-they-need"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-10 by the slow-test helpers: seon.fx/tree-id spawns ~7 git children a call (rev-parse HEAD again when read-source already holds it; two for the plain-index check), seon.fx/inputs ~23 (its tree-id and one git status per submodule pin), and a child spawned from Jolt costs 20-35 ms against ~10 ms from a shell (docs/machine.md#processes; the Xcode git shim, 11.3 ms against 5.1, is bin/env's since 0558694d31). test-runner/program recomputes the checkout's tree id on every call (~7 children for every test that reads the program). Correct: tree-id takes HEAD from its caller when held; inputs reads every pin with one walk of its gitlinks (one git ls-files -s, and a status only of the dirty ones); test-runner/program reads the tree id once a process (the plan's, from tests.edn). Estimate: a program-reading test's children 7 -> 0, a fx/inputs call 23 -> ~3. Proof, red first: a cost proof on fx/inputs counting child processes, constant in the number of pins."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-verdict-caches-size-ledger-counts-a-rewrite-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-10 by a slow-test helper (group B): seon.fx/evict!'s size ledger adds a rewritten verdict path's whole size again, so the ledger drifts upward past the cache's real size and evicts early. Correct: a write's ledger delta is its new size less the old file's. Proof, red first: rewriting one verdict ten times leaves the ledger at its size once."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-write-past-capacity-proof-measures-a-write-past-capacity"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-10 by a slow-test helper (group B): seon.results-test/a-write-past-capacity-counts-the-cache-once never measures a write past capacity: seon.cost/measure keeps its cheapest call, and after the first call evicts every later one is under the bound, so its bytes are flat (~106.7 KB at 10, 100, 1,000) under a linear estimate; a write counting the cache twice while evicting stays green. A test that cannot fail on its claim. Correct: each measured call starts past capacity (its input makes a fresh cache at the bound, as the input of a measured call, not once). Proof: red under a mutation counting the cache twice on eviction."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-fork-item-that-waits-forever-is-ended"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Tests. Since 2026-11-12 a process test runs as any test does, a fork of the agent holding the run's branch, granted in a batch (seon.prove/batch-of); the cold bin/test --shard item and its wall bound (bin/bounded through run-item) are gone. A test's tick budget bounds its work, not a wait: a system test parked on a socket read of its test root (the with-agent teardown that waited on a past end event, Context's 300 s, 2026-11-12) holds its worker until the root dies. Correct: a wait in a test child ends with its value or a typed error; first the known waits (with-agent's teardown, now @(c/kill! sys a)), then a test child parked past a bound stated as data (an estimate x 10), ended after its evidence (bin/evidence), which the owner's clock exceptions allow (docs/clocks.md). Proof, red first: a test that parks forever in a fork item is ended, its evidence kept, and its worker freed. See docs/merging.md#the-test-service."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}} {:seon.task/name "a-systems-items-end-with-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-12 (Optimization, the itemseed trial): bin/system stop ended a worktree system that was proving a tree through its items, while two process items (bin/test --shard, each starting test roots in turn) and a check-step item (bin/check --steps) were running. After the stop they kept running, and the shards kept starting new test roots, until 12 process groups were ended by hand, each verified under the checkout's path. A system's processes end with it (AGENTS.md section 8), but an item's command (seon.fx/run-item through seon.lib/sh, or bin/bounded) is not in the system's process group and is not ended at its stop. Correct: the :item effect owner keeps each running item's process group, and the system's stop ends them all, the same way bin/bounded ends one past its bound, before the system answers its stop. Proof, red first: a system proving a tree with a long process item is stopped; no process of that item's group outlives the stop. See docs/merging.md#a-session-proves-through-root."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-systems-items-end-with-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-git-status-that-prints-nothing-says-why"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen twice on 2026-11-12, both under load in a seed (bin/system seed, seon.system/seed-program! -> seon.fx/inputs -> seon.fx/pin): `git status --porcelain=v2 --branch` exited 0 with empty output for a submodule (reference-code/zstd), whose status a moment later printed its header (detached, f8745da6ff). pin refuses it with a typed throw, so the seed fails and its run reports no tests. Not yet known: whether git printed nothing, or the output was lost between git and seon.fx/git! (lib/sh's pipes under load). Next cheapest observation: git! records the bytes it read and git's exit status beside each call that answers empty; if git's own stdout was non-empty, the loss is in lib/sh (runtime's), else git's. Proof, red first: a git! call whose child writes its output after a delay under a busy reader still answers that output."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-git-call-that-answers-empty-says-why}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner review accepted: the bound is declared on the effect's request schema, a call or scope can only shorten it, Flow enforces ticks and bytes per message, the effects owner enforces wall time and child memory. His changes override the draft: no :seon.fx/workers (the schema says :seon.fx/needs #{:cpu :memory :io} and :seon.fx/exclusive; the one admission decides from cores and memory); shell defaults to :seon.fx/reuse :never unless its request declares its inputs, then :by-reads; effects are routed at Jolt's host primitives (process spawn, file open, socket connect, env read, clock read) inside agent processes, none in root's bootstrap, so interop cannot bypass them; every default bound states its reason from measured runs (one policy, B16); outside reads route automatically; defaults are a malli :default property, overrides documented config keys through the one resolver. What exists: system.edn's :fx map, lib/sh used at 16 sites in fx.clj and the agents' REPL sh, vector's raw ProcessBuilder, bin/bounded, held, evidence, stalls. Design: one entry such as :seon.fx.shell/request carries :seon.fx/perform and a :seon.bound/bound map (ticks, bytes, rss, ms, items); one answer schema :seon.fx/answer, a value or a typed error, always with :seon.bound/spent and :seon.fx/read entries (path and sha-256, or env name and value); a reached bound is :seon.bound/reached; a killed effect says :seon.fx/indeterminate. Owner and worker procs are made from entries naming :seon.fx/perform. Effective bound is the least per key of schema default, request and scope; Flow reads it from the message's :seon.bound/bound, not proc opts (about 5 lines beyond deps' D). Effect functions are detected by reach to seon.fx/ask, so a reload with none runs automatically and one with effects is refused unless accepted (B6). Modeled at a REPL [M]: jolt.process first use cost 55.8M ticks and 603 MB, so the fork server preloads it; a killed child's grandchild survived, so deps' :pgroup and destroy-group are required; env and clock reads cost 0.007 and 0.005 ms, so they mark the run's reads or :never-reusable, no owner round trip; logging is an event on the process's report channel, not an effect; jolt.http.core's own timers fold into the one timer, a streaming reply re-arms it per chunk. Deletes: the :fx map, the :echo test effect in production, per-effect hand lists, \"kind\" in fx.clj, bin/ bound logic. Proofs red first, each its 95 percent in ms. Proving as messages, the process effect pulled forward (owner, 2026-11-14: \"No gates or fences unless it's at boot or an edge ... I want the system to operate via flow\"; runtime and tests agreed): (1) a request :seon.fx.process/request {:seon.process/env {:branch :commit :t}, :seon.fx/run (test names, a check step), :seon.bound/bound {:ticks :ms :rss}, :seon.step/request-id}, answered once by :seon.fx/answer (verdicts, or a typed error, :seon.bound/reached among them). (2) One effects owner proc in root's graph: its in-port a fixed buffer (a full one is the back-pressure), admitting from cores and free memory (seon.prove's admission moves here; no :workers count); a root-booting test weighs the whole admission until its peak is measured. (3) Each admitted run is a worker: the agent holding that env forks a test child (seon.program/hold-env! a no-op); another env is an agent spawned on that branch, and a pin or native difference answers :seon.process/env-mismatch, which asks a build effect; results stream up the link; the end is :seon.process/end; the deadline is one put-after! message that kills the group and answers :seon.bound/reached, never a cached red. (4) A dead test child is an error as data on its answer (:seon.process/end with its signal), never a rerun of its shard in a new child: run-children!'s respawn goes in the same landing. (5) Verdicts are cached only from a process whose hold-env! held the request's env. (6) A merge's check is the same request on the candidate branch's env: no worktree, no gate rebuild. Deletes: the merge gate's worktree and rebuild, :workers 1 (system.edn), prove.clj's grant and admission (DELETIONS rows 53-55). Runtime builds the owner proc and its channels; tests the run's request, the child's answer and the merge check."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-model-answer-is-recorded-never-reused seon.fx-test/a-step-is-bounded-by-its-messages-bound seon.fx-test/an-outside-read-is-recorded-and-reused-while-its-digest-holds seon.fx-test/a-shell-past-its-ms-is-killed-whole-and-may-have-happened seon.fx-test/a-reload-with-no-effect-runs-and-one-with-an-effect-is-refused-unless-accepted seon.fx-test/an-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry seon.fx-test/a-call-can-only-shorten-its-bound seon.fx-test/a-child-memory-is-measured-at-exit}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.step/every-seon-proc-follows-one-flow-standard-with-a-runnable-example-each"}}
 {:seon.task/name "runtime-for-reads-the-pins-from-the-branch", :seon.task/plan "Agreed with runtime and the owner, 2026-11-14 (plans/deps-runtime-for.md). What exists: three guesses at the runtime a tree needs (bin/build current, test-runner/base-products, bin/land's bin/build jolt) and the ls-tree read of gitlinks (src/seon/fx.clj:175-214). Change: seon.fx/runtime-for [env] reads the pins from the branch's db (the gitlink entities), compares target/<step>.stamp with them, and answers the built runtime, or builds it, or :seon.process/env-mismatch naming the fields (data's error schema); runtime's start-process! calls it as the one check. Deletes: bin/build current, test-runner/base-products, the ls-tree read. Done: every runner asks runtime-for; a law goes red on one that execs target/seon or target/jolt without it. docs/merging.md#a-branch-lands-with-binland.", :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-process-on-another-runtime-answers-env-mismatch}, :seon.task/needs #{"seon.source/an-import-writes-each-gitlink-as-an-entity"}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-scripts-environment-is-stated-in-one-place", :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-14 (deps): a shell that had sourced bin/env ran bin/jolt-gates, its JOLT_COMPILER_OPTIONS {:image-fn-forms false} reached seven Jolt gates and turned them red, and its HOME broke a git commit. bin/jolt-gates now clears JOLT_ and SEON_ variables itself, a per-script fix. Needed: what environment each script may take is stated in one place and enforced there, so no run inherits its caller's shell: plan first, citing bin/env and bin/jolt-gates. Done: a law goes red on a script that reads a variable the one statement does not give it, and bin/jolt-gates' own clearing is deleted. docs/machine.md#processes.", :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-script-takes-only-the-environment-it-is-given}}]
